Strict alignment: prove accesses through locals aligned (applies after perf-39).

perf-39 proves an address aligned only from the tree that forms it, so a byref held in a
local - a CSE temp of "array data + i * 32", the base an inlinee copies its pointer argument
to, a pointer loaded once and offset many times - loses its provenance, and every access
through it gets the andi/bnez test with a byte-wise slow path. On SP1 that test sits in
front of most EVM stack and Keccak lane accesses of the Nethermind guest.

Lowering now runs ComputeAlignedLocals before it lowers any block: for every local whose
definitions are all GT_STORE_LCL_VAR (not a parameter, not address-exposed, no GT_LCL_ADDR
or partial store, not a promoted struct or field) it finds, as an optimistic fixed point,
an alignment every stored value has. The alignment proof becomes KnownAlignmentLog2, which
returns a power of two instead of a yes/no for one width and reads those locals: a sum,
difference or OR keeps the weaker operand's alignment, a constant shift adds to it, a
product adds both, an AND keeps the stronger one (masking only clears bits) and an
integral cast keeps it. Object references, LCL_ADDR, handles and constants are as before.

The AND rule lets managed code establish alignment it knows but the JIT cannot see: a
byref parameter rounded down to the boundary it is already on, (p & ~31), is free to
compute once and proves every access off it.

Nethermind stateless guest on SP1 (sp1-runner v6.5.0), cycles:
  block 26055159: 169,834,110 -> 166,130,276 (-2.2%) with this patch alone,
                  -> 141,125,404 (-16.9%) with the guest rounding its stack and Keccak bases
  block 26112793: 409,369,758 -> 399,352,385 (-2.4%) alone, -> 320,921,838 (-21.6%)
Outputs identical on 11 mainnet blocks. Only builds with JitRiscV64StrictAlign change.

